 "observations": [
  "The page number «189» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 75», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The title (L01), «Defectus lunares1) spatio propinquo (CON)», the lunar eclipses at the near distance, in bold, the sigla in roman; a speck before «Defectus», not transcribed. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the title in two lines, «C 84v, O 141r,» and «N 46v.», the letters raised, the comma after «141r» printed in two pieces; here as right margin notes beside L01 and L02.",
  "The table: as on pp. 187-188 (JP2 0219-0220), fourteen columns: «Argumentum latitudinis» over «Gr.», «Min.», «Gr.», «Min.»; «Digiti defectus» over «Dig.», «Min.»; «Dakaicae2) casus» and «Dakaicae2) dimidiae morae4)», each over «Min.», «Sec.»; «Tabula proportionis3)» over «Partitiones diversitatum», two columns «Gr.», and «Dakaicae2) superationum», «Min.», «Sec». Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L02-L05); the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. The first ten columns end with the row of 0° 0′ (L33); the table of proportion continues to 180° (L35).",
  "L06-L33. The arguments of latitude 13° 17′, 13° 0′ and then every half degree to 0° 0′, and their supplements, 166° 43′ to 180° 0′, with the readings of notes a and b: the lunar eclipses at the least distance of the moon (Suter, pp. 86-88, JP2 0118-0120). Checked: the central row gives R + r = 34′ 34″ + 27′ 27″ = 62′ 1″ and R − r = 27′ 27″, the radius of the moon r = 17′ 17″, the value of Tab. 66 at 180° of anomaly (p. 180, JP2 0212), and the radius of the shadow R = 44′ 44″; the casus at 0° is the diameter of the moon, 34′ 34″. With the latitude β = 4° 30′ · sin x the limit of the eclipse, β = R + r, falls at 13° 16.7′, the 13° 17′ of the first row, and the totality begins at β = R − r, at 5° 50′. In the rows of total eclipse, from 5° 0′ to 0° 0′, the casus with the half mora, √((R + r)² − β²), agrees within 4″, and the half mora, √((R − r)² − β²), within 5″ except at 3° 0′ (L27), 23′ 52″ for 23′ 32″, 20″ more (the steps of the column 1′ 55″ and 57″, computed 1′ 35″ and 1′ 16″), where Tab. 76 (p. 190, JP2 0222) has 23′ 32″: see the critical note. The casus of the partial eclipses agrees within 26″ except at 10° 30′ and 10° 0′ (L12-L13), 37′ 0″ and 41′ 27″ for 37′ 45″ and 40′ 36″. At 5° 30′ (L22), where the half mora begins with 9′ 7″ (9′ 9″ computed), the casus has 57′ 14″, 52″ more than the whole time to the middle of the eclipse, √((R + r)² − β²) = 56′ 22″, for 47′ 12″, the difference of the two, where Tab. 76 has 47′ 14″: see the critical note.",
  "L06-L33, the digits. Checked: (R + r − β) · 12 : 2r gives 21 dig. 31.8′ at 0°, the 21 dig. 31′ of L33, and agrees within 3′ in every row except at 10° 30′ (4 dig. 58′ for 4 dig. 26.9′, 31′ more), 10° 0′ (14′ more), 9° 30′ (6′), 7° 30′ (8′), 7° 0′ (5′), 5° 30′ (12 dig. 55′ for 12 dig. 32.7′, 22′ more), 5° 0′ (5′) and 4° 30′ (10′). The digits grow by 40′ to 55′ for each half degree of the argument except from 11° 0′ to 10° 0′, 3 dig. 36′, 4 dig. 58′, 5 dig. 29′ (82′ and 31′), and from 6° 0′ to 5° 0′, 11 dig. 43′, 12 dig. 55′, 13 dig. 27′ (72′ and 32′); Tab. 76 has 4 dig. 38′ and 12 dig. 35′ at the same distances from the node, which give the steps 62′ and 51′, and 52′ and 52′: see the critical notes. Suter's example, the argument 359° 12′ (p. 86, JP2 0118, L27-L30), lies 0° 48′ from the node: between 0° 30′ and 1° 0′ the interpolation gives 20 dig. 13.6′, 34′ 45.4″ and 27′ 7.6″, the 20 dig. 14′, 34′ 45″ and 27′ 8″ that Suter takes from Table 76 (p. 87, JP2 0119, L02), with C's 16 at 0° 30′ (note f; with O's 26 the half mora would be 27′ 11.6″).",
  "L06-L33, continued. The radius of the shadow implied by the table, 44′ 44″, is 28″ more than the difference of the last two columns of Tab. 66 at 180°, 57′ 4″ − 12′ 48″ = 44′ 16″, which Suter takes for the radius of the shadow (p. 90, JP2 0122, L11-L18); at the greatest distance Tab. 73 (p. 187) implies 36′ 0″, where Tab. 61 has 48′ 19″ − 11′ 52″ = 36′ 27″ at 1° of anomaly.",
  "L06-L35, the table of proportion: the anomalies 122°-180° and 238°-180°, continuing p. 188 (120°, 44′ 0″), to 60′ 0″ at 180°, the whole difference between the near and the far values. Checked: within 11″ of the share of the decrease of the moon's distance on its epicycle of 5p 10′, as on pp. 187-188, most at 132°-136°; the steps fall from 58″ to 2″.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: the edition's readings agree with the computation at 13° 17′ (note b: 166° 43′, against the 30 of C and O, is 180° less 13° 17′), at 0° 0′ (note a: the 180 of O and N against C's 179), at 3° 0′ (note d: the edition's 16 dig. 38′ against the 15 of C and O; 16 dig. 37.4′ computed) and at 5° 0′ (note e: O's 43′ 13″ against C's 58; 43′ 14.7″ computed). At 0° 30′ (note f) C's 27′ 16″ and O's 27′ 26″ lie 4.9″ and 5.1″ from the computation, 27′ 20.9″; Suter's example agrees with C's. At 10° 30′ (note c) the 4 dig. 58′ of O and N and C's 3 dig. 58′ lie 31′ above and 29′ below the computation, 4 dig. 26.9′.",
  "Notes 1-4: «lunaris» (C and O) for «lunares»; N's «Minuta» for «Dakaicae»; O's «Pagina proportionis», C omits the head; N's «mediae morae» for «dimidiae morae». Italics: «om.». N01-N02: «pro-portionis» divided at the end of the line. N02, the comma at the end of the numbered notes: see the critical note."
 ],
